# Rank 1405 / CVE-2025-11695 (GHSA-3p6w-gv5g-xjw9, CWE-295):
# mongodb/mongo-rust-driver parses `tlsInsecure` out of a connection string with
# its boolean inverted. `ConnectionString::parse_option_pair` handled the option
# in the same match arm as its narrower sibling and negated the value for one of
# them:
#
#   k @ "tlsinsecure" | k @ "tlsallowinvalidcertificates" => {
#       let val = get_bool!(value, k);
#       let allow_invalid_certificates =
#           if k == "tlsinsecure" { !val } else { val };   // options.rs:2070
#       ...
#       Some(Tls::Enabled(ref mut options)) => {
#           options.allow_invalid_certificates = Some(allow_invalid_certificates);
#       }
#   }
#
# Both the option and the field it lands in are permissive-polarity: the option
# name asserts insecurity and the field name asserts that invalid certificates
# are accepted. Negating between them flips the meaning of the setting. So a URI
# carrying `?tls=true&tlsInsecure=false` -- an application explicitly declining
# to relax TLS -- produced `allow_invalid_certificates = Some(true)`, and every
# connection accepted any certificate the peer presented, while
# `tlsInsecure=true` turned validation on. The attacker is a MITM on the
# driver-to-server link, or a hostile server: with validation off it can present
# a self-signed certificate for any name and read or rewrite the wire protocol,
# including the SCRAM exchange. Nothing in the application is misused; the
# application asked for the secure setting and got the insecure one.
#
# 21ed6aeeea (the 3.2.5 cherry-pick of #1453) splits the arm in two, drops the
# negation, and makes the option mean what its documentation says:
#
#   TLS_INSECURE => {
#       let val = get_bool!(value, key);
#       self.tls_insecure = Some(val);
#       match self.tls.get_or_insert_with(|| Tls::Enabled(Default::default())) {
#           Tls::Enabled(ref mut options) => {
#               options.allow_invalid_certificates = Some(val);
#               #[cfg(feature = "openssl-tls")]
#               { options.allow_invalid_hostnames = Some(val); }
#           }
#           Tls::Disabled => return Err(...),
#       }
#   }
#
# and adds the URI-spec conflict errors (`tlsInsecure` together with
# `tlsAllowInvalidCertificates`, or with `tls=false`) plus case-insensitive
# duplicate-key detection. The regression test in src/client/options/test.rs
# asserts the polarity in both directions.
#
# The pool row is correct in every field. 21ed6aeeea is a real, readable,
# non-merge commit; the negated derivation is present at its parent
# (src/client/options.rs:2070) and the remediation is present at the commit
# itself, so both halves of the 1390 check hold. No corpus correction.
#
# What fires today. `timeout 180 ./bin/vyql scan --cache off --max-ram 6GB` over
# both real checkouts reports the same 49 findings at or above high, byte-stable
# across three runs each, with an identical per-rule split before and after:
# VYQL-MEM-006 x185, VYQL-DOS-042 x25, VYQL-CFG-014 x18, VYQL-MEM-005 x8,
# VYQL-PATH-001 x7, and singletons below. Exactly four of them land in
# src/client/options.rs and all four are the host-list parser, not the option
# parser: three VYQL-MEM-005 rows on the `s[..]`/`hosts[..]` slices at 1403,
# 1421 and 1599 and one VYQL-CFG-014 on the TrustBoundary at 1604, each shifted
# four lines by the change and otherwise identical. `scan -flags only` adds eight
# code.IndexAccess flags before and seven after, again all subscripts. Nothing
# labels the TLS arm in either revision -- the pattern ranks 1370, 1371, 1378
# and 1393 recorded holds once more: findings in the file are not coverage.
#
# Vocabulary is checked for reuse before anything else. code.CertValidationDisabled
# (VYQL-CFG-004, CWE-295) is the right existing home and is what the measurements
# below emit; its Rust binding today is `danger_accept_invalid_certs(true)`, a
# literal-argument call, which is the consumer side of this weakness and not the
# parser side. The other CWE-295 concepts are read and rejected on shape:
# core.CertificateValidation is a check concept for the neutralising control,
# code.ProxyRedirectCertVerificationBypass (VYQL-CFG-023) is about a redirect
# hop, code.CertValidationDependency is SCA, code.SnsCertificateUrlTrustBypass
# and code.JavaMailSmtpSslIdentityVerificationMissing are unrelated protocols.
# Nothing anywhere in vyql/ontology, vyql/review or vyql/packs expresses the
# weakness class this CVE actually is -- a security setting assigned the logical
# negation of the option that requests it. No concept and no rule ID is minted
# here, because the measurements say nothing discriminating can be built on one.
#
# ATTENTION, engine gap: the two facts that would have to meet cannot meet.
#
# (1) The negation is a node, and a node carries no function facts. `code.Unary`
#     with path `__unary.not` exists at options.rs:2070 and a presence binding
#     on `node.kind == "unary"` labels it -- 8 such nodes in the vulnerable file,
#     9 in the patched one, the extra being the fixed revision's own `if !tls` guard. But
#     adding any `node.context.*` clause to that binding drops it to zero in both
#     revisions: context predicates read the matched node's own `str_args`
#     (`flagContextTokenValuePredicate`, internal/bindings/bindings_match.go:1520),
#     and a Unary node has none. Measured with `node.context.language == "rust"`,
#     `node.context.name == "parse_option_pair"` and
#     `node.context.selector contains "allow_invalid_certificates"`: 0 and 0.
#     So the negation cannot be qualified by anything about where it sits, and
#     both `!` unaries in this function are indistinguishable.
#
# (2) The destination is a function fact, and it has the value erased.
#     `rsStructuredContextTokens` (internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/rust.go:594)
#     emits `assign:` only for `assignment_expression`, and `atom()` collapses a
#     call-shaped right-hand side to its dotted callee path. Both revisions
#     therefore emit the *same* token, `assign:options.allow_invalid_certificates=Some`,
#     and the `let` that actually holds the negation emits no `assign:` token at
#     all because `let_declaration` is not in that switch. There is no unary or
#     negation prefix in the Rust token set (the switch handles
#     assignment_expression, call_expression, field_expression and match_arm).
#
# (3) The destination is absent from the USG as well. `(*rsConv).exprStmt`
#     (internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/rust.go:911) builds an `nir.Assign`
#     only when the assignment's left-hand side is a bare `identifier`; a
#     `field_expression` target is discarded and the statement becomes a bare
#     `ExprStmt` of the right-hand side. Case (d) below is the minimal pair:
#     `vyql graph` over it shows `Unary path=__unary.not` for both functions,
#     `Unary --FLOWS--> Arg --FLOWS--> Call path=Some` for the field-target one,
#     and no node anywhere naming `allow_invalid_certificates`. The value's
#     destination simply is not in the graph, so "a negation reaches a
#     certificate-relaxation setting" is not a statement the query language can
#     make about Rust today.
#
# This is not the 1364/1377/1397/1399 delegated-check family and that is tested,
# not assumed: the remediation here is inline in the same function the weakness
# is in, no check moves into a sibling, and no call needs resolving. Landing the
# caller-side control attribution those ranks are blocked on would change nothing
# for this rank. Rank 1380's counterweight also comes out negative -- the patched
# arm's API-documented content (`get_bool` on the URI value, an assignment to
# `allow_invalid_certificates`) is present in the vulnerable arm too.
#
# The one clause that does separate the revisions is built and then rejected,
# with the rename test the preamble prescribes. `node.scope == "function" and
# node.context.matchArm contains "tlsinsecure" and node.context.assign contains
# "allow_invalid_certificates"` scores 1 on the vulnerable file and 0 on the
# patched one -- but it separates them only because the fixed revision replaced the string
# literal `"tlsinsecure"` with the constant `TLS_INSECURE` in the arm pattern.
# Respelling the patched arms with their original literals, changing nothing else
# about the remediation, brings the flag straight back: 1 on fully-fixed code.
# That is the fixing commit's own layout as the discriminator, which is standing
# rejection grounds, and it is also wrong in the field -- any driver that spells
# the arm with a literal and gets the polarity right would be flagged.
#
# The shape is rare enough that precision is never the obstacle. Across the 120
# rank clones under /tmp/cve-work (1338 Rust files), 22 files mention a
# certificate-relaxation field at all, 16 mention a `tlsInsecure`-family option
# name, and a grep for a relaxation field assigned a negated expression returns
# exactly one hit outside this rank's own scratch copies: options.rs:2070, the
# CVE. There is a real detection here and nothing to write it with.
#
# The gap is not judged mechanical. Making `exprStmt` keep a `field_expression`
# target would create new Assign nodes and new flow edges in every Rust scan,
# which is a solver-wide dataflow change rather than a confined correction, and
# even with it the negation would still have to be correlated through the `Some`
# wrapper and the intervening `let` to the target -- a second capability that
# does not exist and is not measured.
#
# The four cases are the pair, plus the two instances that make it a pair. (a)
# and (b) are the real revisions and record the miss. (c) is the correct polarity
# conversion in the same vocabulary and shows why "a negation into a relaxation
# field" cannot itself be the key. (d) is the frontend minimal pair for (3).
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap, all four cases rejected. (c) and
# (d) are correct code and must remain rejects.

test "rank1405: (a) a connection-string option whose name asserts insecurity has its boolean negated on the way into the field that accepts invalid certificates, so declining to relax TLS disables validation"
  lang rust
  file src/client/options.rs
  reject VYQL-CFG-004
  reject VYQL-CFG-023
  code
  ```
  use crate::error::{Error, ErrorKind, Result};
  use crate::options::{Tls, TlsOptions};

  pub struct ConnectionString {
      pub tls: Option<Tls>,
      pub tls_insecure: Option<bool>,
  }

  macro_rules! get_bool {
      ($value:expr, $option:expr) => {
          match $value {
              "true" => true,
              "false" => false,
              _ => {
                  return Err(ErrorKind::InvalidArgument {
                      message: format!("connection string `{}` option must be a boolean", $option),
                  }
                  .into())
              }
          }
      };
  }

  impl ConnectionString {
      fn parse_option_pair(&mut self, key: &str, value: &str) -> Result<()> {
          match key {
              k @ "tls" | k @ "ssl" => {
                  let tls = get_bool!(value, k);
                  if self.tls.is_none() {
                      let tls = if tls {
                          Tls::Enabled(Default::default())
                      } else {
                          Tls::Disabled
                      };
                      self.tls = Some(tls);
                  }
              }
              k @ "tlsinsecure" | k @ "tlsallowinvalidcertificates" => {
                  let val = get_bool!(value, k);

                  let allow_invalid_certificates = if k == "tlsinsecure" { !val } else { val };

                  match self.tls {
                      Some(Tls::Disabled) => {
                          return Err(ErrorKind::InvalidArgument {
                              message: "'tlsInsecure' can't be set if tls=false".into(),
                          }
                          .into())
                      }
                      Some(Tls::Enabled(ref mut options)) => {
                          options.allow_invalid_certificates = Some(allow_invalid_certificates);
                      }
                      None => {
                          self.tls = Some(Tls::Enabled(
                              TlsOptions::builder()
                                  .allow_invalid_certificates(allow_invalid_certificates)
                                  .build(),
                          ))
                      }
                  }
              }
              _ => {}
          }
          Ok(())
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1405: (b) the fixed revision gives the insecure option its own arm and stores the parsed value with its polarity intact"
  lang rust
  file src/client/options.rs
  reject VYQL-CFG-004
  reject VYQL-CFG-023
  code
  ```
  use crate::error::{Error, ErrorKind, Result};
  use crate::options::{Tls, TlsOptions};

  const TLS_INSECURE: &str = "tlsinsecure";
  const TLS_ALLOW_INVALID_CERTIFICATES: &str = "tlsallowinvalidcertificates";

  pub struct ConnectionString {
      pub tls: Option<Tls>,
      pub tls_insecure: Option<bool>,
  }

  macro_rules! get_bool {
      ($value:expr, $option:expr) => {
          match $value {
              "true" => true,
              "false" => false,
              _ => {
                  return Err(Error::invalid_argument(format!(
                      "connection string `{}` option must be a boolean",
                      $option
                  )))
              }
          }
      };
  }

  impl ConnectionString {
      fn parse_option_pair(&mut self, key: &str, value: &str) -> Result<()> {
          match key {
              k @ "tls" | k @ "ssl" => {
                  let tls = get_bool!(value, k);
                  match self.tls {
                      Some(Tls::Enabled(_)) if !tls => {
                          return Err(Error::invalid_argument(
                              "cannot set {key}={tls} if other TLS options are set",
                          ))
                      }
                      Some(Tls::Disabled) if tls => {
                          return Err(Error::invalid_argument(
                              "cannot set {key}={tls} if TLS is disabled",
                          ))
                      }
                      None => {
                          if tls {
                              self.tls = Some(Tls::Enabled(Default::default()))
                          } else {
                              self.tls = Some(Tls::Disabled)
                          }
                      }
                      _ => {}
                  }
              }
              TLS_INSECURE => {
                  let val = get_bool!(value, key);
                  self.tls_insecure = Some(val);

                  match self
                      .tls
                      .get_or_insert_with(|| Tls::Enabled(Default::default()))
                  {
                      Tls::Enabled(ref mut options) => {
                          options.allow_invalid_certificates = Some(val);
                          options.allow_invalid_hostnames = Some(val);
                      }
                      Tls::Disabled => {
                          return Err(Error::invalid_argument(format!(
                              "cannot set {key} when TLS is disabled"
                          )));
                      }
                  }
              }
              TLS_ALLOW_INVALID_CERTIFICATES => {
                  let val = get_bool!(value, key);

                  match self
                      .tls
                      .get_or_insert_with(|| Tls::Enabled(Default::default()))
                  {
                      Tls::Enabled(ref mut options) => {
                          options.allow_invalid_certificates = Some(val);
                      }
                      Tls::Disabled => {
                          return Err(Error::invalid_argument(format!(
                              "cannot set {key} when TLS is disabled"
                          )))
                      }
                  }
              }
              _ => {}
          }
          Ok(())
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1405: (c) a relaxation field assigned the negation of a strict-polarity option is the correct conversion and must not be flagged"
  lang rust
  file src/runtime/tls_rustls.rs
  reject VYQL-CFG-004
  reject VYQL-CFG-023
  code
  ```
  use crate::error::Result;
  use crate::options::TlsOptions;

  pub struct AppConfig {
      pub verify_server_certificate: bool,
      pub verify_server_hostname: bool,
  }

  pub fn tls_options_from_config(config: &AppConfig) -> Result<TlsOptions> {
      let mut options = TlsOptions::default();

      options.allow_invalid_certificates = Some(!config.verify_server_certificate);
      options.allow_invalid_hostnames = Some(!config.verify_server_hostname);

      Ok(options)
  }
  ```

test "rank1405: (d) the frontend pair: a negation stored through a bare identifier and the same negation stored into a struct field, whose target the Rust lowering discards"
  lang rust
  file src/client/options/pair.rs
  reject VYQL-CFG-004
  reject VYQL-CFG-023
  code
  ```
  pub struct TlsOptions {
      pub allow_invalid_certificates: Option<bool>,
  }

  fn identifier_target(val: bool) -> bool {
      let mut allow_invalid_certificates = false;
      allow_invalid_certificates = !val;
      allow_invalid_certificates
  }

  fn field_target(options: &mut TlsOptions, val: bool) {
      options.allow_invalid_certificates = Some(!val);
  }
  ```
